# CMS Admin Filters

Every Admin **list/Index** screen uses one shared filter UX. Do not invent per-page search bars.

## UX

- Toolbar shows a **filter icon button** (`fas fa-filter`), not an always-visible search input.
- Click opens a **popup panel** with that page’s filters.
- Active filters show a small **dot badge** on the icon.
- Panel actions: **اعمال فیلتر** (submit GET) and **پاک کردن** (link to Index without filter query).

## Markup

Use the shared partial in the toolbar (usually after primary actions):

```cshtml
<div class="admin-toolbar">
  <a class="btn btn-primary" asp-action="Create">…</a>
  @{
    ViewData["SearchPlaceholder"] = Localizer["SearchPosts"].Value;
    // Optional page-specific fields:
    // ViewData["FilterFieldsPartial"] = "_PostFilters";
    // ViewBag.HasActiveFilters = hasSearch || hasStatus;
  }
  <partial name="_FilterPanel" />
</div>
```

- Default field: text search `q` (label from `ViewData["SearchPlaceholder"]`).
- Extra fields: set `ViewData["FilterFieldsPartial"]` to a page partial rendered **inside** the filter form.
- Hide default search only when needed: `ViewData["FilterHideSearch"] = true`.
- Set `ViewBag.HasActiveFilters = true` when any non-`q` filter is active (or leave unset if only `q` matters).

## Controllers

- Filters are **GET** query params (`q`, `status`, …). Reset `page` to 1 when filters change (omit `page` from the form).
- Pass `ViewBag.Search = q` (and other filter values) so the panel can rebind.
- Empty-state copy: use `NoSearchResults` when filters are active and the list is empty.

## Do Not

- Put a permanent search input in the toolbar.
- Duplicate filter popup markup per module — extend `_FilterPanel` / a fields partial.
- Use POST for list filtering.
- Mix public-site filter UI with Admin (`wwwroot/admin` only).
